  "measures": [],
  "notes": "A 300 nm silica sphere at the focus of an NA 0.8 objective (an ideal lens, f = 10 mm), in 100 mW of 1550 nm light polarised in the bench plane. The sphere is a Particle part: the light passes it untouched, and what it scatters goes to the parts that can see it. A like lens ahead collects the forward scatter with the unscattered beam and sends both to a split detector, which reads the sum and the difference of its halves: move the particle across the focus (its y, a few tens of nanometres) and Right less left follows it. A second lens at 90° collects NA 0.5 of the side scatter onto a detector of its own. Select the particle and open its Scattering tab for its cross-sections, the power it scatters, the share each part catches and what none does. The faint fan is the pattern in the bench plane: with the light polarised in the plane almost none goes along the polarisation, which is why the side detector reads so little. Turn the view to 3D to see the fan over the whole sphere. The Particle tab switches the fan, or all of the scattered light, off.",
  "style": {}
